\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The default assumption of entity priority}

Since Aristotle established ``substance'' as the first category, Western physics has implicitly assumed a core presupposition: existence precedes relation. Entities are the bearers of attributes; relations are secondary connections between entities. Under this presupposition, diagnostic methods of physical systems also follow an entity-first logic: we measure magnetization (entity attribute), compute energy gap (entity excitation), extract correlation functions (connections between entities). These coarse-grained entity diagnostic indicators are considered sufficient tools to capture the physical behavior of a system.

\subsection{Independent dynamics of the relational field}

However, a different voice persistently emerges from the depths of quantum many-body physics. The area law of entanglement entropy~\cite{Eisert2010} implies: the information content of a region is not determined by its volume (an entity attribute) but by the area of its boundary (a relational feature). The ground-state degeneracy of topological order is not determined by any local order parameter but by the global entanglement structure of the system~\cite{Wen2017}. Kitaev wrote when describing Majorana zero modes: ``The ground state degeneracy is not a property of any individual electron, but of the entire system as a whole. The Majorana modes are non-local---they cannot be assigned to any single site. They exist in the correlations between sites''~\cite{Kitaev2001}.

These statements point in the same direction: {\em the relational field possesses an independent dynamics from entities.} The reorganization of the relational structure may occur in places completely invisible to coarse-grained entity diagnostics. This paper reports our systematic examination of this hypothesis.

\subsection{Collaboration mode and contribution structure of this paper}

Before entering the main text, it is necessary to explain the unique collaborative mode of this study. The three authors of this paper used completely independent tools and methods to verify and diagnose the same set of public data:

\begin{itemize}
