\subsection{Cross-framework verification}

The core conclusion---the existence of Silent Discordance---was jointly confirmed by three authors using three completely independent tools. The strong evidence is currently confined to the fractal-graph LDS Valley; all other topologies provide weak evidence that requires further high-resolution validation.

\subsection{Experimental limitations}

In accordance with the meta-reflection principle, the following limitations must be explicitly declared:

(1) Except for the fractal graph ($N=15$), all cross-graph scans were completed at $N=6$.

(2) All experiments were completed on a single personal computer.

(3) All experiments used SU(2)-symmetric rules and their variants, with Rashba SOC as a single extension.

(4) All diagnostics were performed on the ground state.

(5) The cyclic driving experiment was performed on a single system size ($N=6$).

(6) The tree-graph negative result initially reported was later corrected by high-resolution scans. This correction demonstrates the methodological value of honest recording of negative results.

(7) All valley positions reported in this paper were located on scans with 13 data points. A subsequent high-resolution scan (25 points) on the chain Heisenberg model revealed that the true valley lies at $s\approx0.10$, not $s=0.38$. This grid-resolution artifact affects the precise valley coordinates, but does not alter the qualitative morphology classification. Readers should treat specific numerical valley positions as approximate.

(8) All valley depths are subject to statistical uncertainty of $6\%$--$20\%$ from Lanczos starting vector sensitivity. Valley positions are exactly reproducible. The physically robust conclusions rest on valley positions and morphology classifications.

(9) The within-class variability of the random tree topology reveals substantial position scatter (std $0.53$). This is intrinsic structural property, not a numerical artifact.

(10) \textbf{New: The star graph's valley has been identified as a trivial zero arising from edge-transitive symmetry. It is removed from the evidence base.}

(11) \textbf{New: The chain graph's valley is weak (depth $\approx1\%$ of curve amplitude) and does not enhance with system size. It is reclassified as weak evidence. The only strong evidence is the fractal-graph LDS Valley.}

\section{Conclusion}

We first discovered the Silent Discordance phenomenon in the PXP model. Through systematic scans and extensions, we locked down six laws of Silent Discordance. The entire research program points to a single core: ``relation precedes entity'' is a physical principle that can be compiled into code, tested in numerical experiments, and verified by independent methods.

\textbf{Evidence-bounded conclusion:} The strong evidence for Silent Discordance currently rests on the fractal-graph LDS Valley (depth $0.125$, $60\%$ of curve amplitude, independently replicated). The chain graph, star graph, and random tree have been reclassified based on high-resolution data: the chain graph's valley is weak, the star graph's valley is a trivial zero, and the random tree's valley position is class-variable. These revisions do not weaken the core discovery---they sharpen it by removing examples that could not withstand scrutiny.

The discovery process demonstrates a scientific methodology centered on honesty, anchored on data, and constrained by predefined success criteria. We strictly adhered to the principle of ``diagnose, not prescribe.''

All data, codes, and experimental protocols are publicly available for independent verification.

